          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "glog-fc9d6818-f9ee-4a0f-803b-36f4f13b375e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **NPM-1088403** in the `moment-timezone` package version `0.5.34` is a security issue that affects applications using this library for handling time zone data. This vulnerability could potentially allow an attacker to manipulate time zone data, leading to incorrect time calculations and possibly affecting application logic that relies on accurate time data.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade the Package**: The most effective way to mitigate this vulnerability is to upgrade to a later version of `moment-timezone` where the issue has been resolved. Check the [moment-timezone GitHub repository](https://github.com/moment/moment-timezone) for the latest stable release.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Ensure that any input related to time zones is validated and sanitized to prevent manipulation.\n\n3. **Monitor Dependencies**: Regularly check for updates and security advisories for all dependencies in your project.\n\n4. **Use Alternative Libraries**: Consider using alternative libraries that provide similar functionality with better security practices, if upgrading is not feasible.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider implementing a temporary fix by validating and sanitizing all time zone data inputs. For example:\n\n```javascript\nconst moment = require('moment-timezone');\n\n// Example of validating time zone input\nfunction isValidTimeZone(tz) {\n    return moment.tz.names().includes(tz);\n}\n\nconst userTimeZone = 'America/New_York'; // Example input\nif (isValidTimeZone(userTimeZone)) {\n    const time = moment.tz('2023-10-01 12:00', userTimeZone);\n    console.log(time.format());\n} else {\n    console.error('Invalid time zone');\n}\n```\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "NPM-1088403/pkg:npm/moment-timezone@0.5.34"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-7df057c7-3d50-458d-be0a-db1c9d20f18f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **NPM-1088402** in the `moment-timezone` package version `0.5.34` is a security issue that affects applications using this library for handling time zone data. This vulnerability could potentially allow an attacker to exploit the way time zone data is processed, leading to incorrect time calculations or even application crashes. The specific details of the vulnerability are not publicly disclosed, but it is crucial to address it to maintain the integrity and reliability of applications relying on `moment-timezone`.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade the Package**: The most effective way to mitigate this vulnerability is to upgrade to a later version of `moment-timezone` where the issue has been resolved. Check the [moment-timezone GitHub repository](https://github.com/moment/moment-timezone) for the latest stable release.\n\n2. **Review Dependency Tree**: Ensure that no other dependencies are using the vulnerable version of `moment-timezone`. Use tools like `npm audit` to identify and resolve such issues.\n\n3. **Implement Input Validation**: As a general security practice, ensure that all inputs related to date and time are validated and sanitized before processing.\n\n4. **Monitor for Updates**: Keep track of security advisories related to `moment-timezone` and other dependencies in your project.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWhile the specific code changes to fix the vulnerability are not provided, the general recommendation is to:\n\n- **Update to a Secure Version**: Modify your `package.json` to require a version of `moment-timezone` that is not affected by this vulnerability. For example:\n\n  ```json\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"moment-timezone\": \"^0.5.35\"\n  }\n  ```\n\n- **Rebuild Your Project**: After updating the version, run `npm install` to update the package lock file and rebuild your project with the secure version.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- **OWASP Resources**:\n  - [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n  - [OWASP Dependency-Check](https://owasp.org/www-project-dependency-check/)\n\n- **Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)**:\n  - [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n\nThese resources provide additional guidance on secure coding practices and dependency management to help prevent similar vulnerabilities in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "NPM-1088402/pkg:npm/moment-timezone@0.5.34"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-e416a475-11b4-4a25-a406-493547f41e92",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2022-38900** affects the `decode-uri-component` package version `0.2.0` in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability arises from improper handling of certain input strings, which can lead to unexpected behavior or potential security risks such as Denial of Service (DoS) or information exposure. The issue is rooted in the way the package decodes URI components, potentially allowing attackers to exploit this flaw by crafting malicious input that the package fails to handle correctly.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider the following steps:\n\n1. **Upgrade the Package**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade `decode-uri-component` to a version where the vulnerability is patched. Check the package repository or npm advisory for the latest secure version.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that only safe and expected input is processed by the application. This can help prevent malformed input from reaching vulnerable code paths.\n\n3. **Monitor Dependencies**: Regularly audit and update your project's dependencies to ensure that you are using the latest and most secure versions available.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a temporary fix by modifying the source code to handle edge cases more robustly. For example, ensure that the decoding function properly checks and sanitizes input before processing it. However, this should be considered a temporary measure until a proper upgrade can be performed.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet Series](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\nThis vulnerability is related to the following CWE:\n\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n\nPlease ensure to verify the links and their accessibility for anonymous users as per your request."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2022-38900/pkg:npm/decode-uri-component@0.2.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-7f1b3192-6315-40f6-b4d0-b69fb6aee3ed",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```markdown\n### Description\n\n**Vulnerability ID:** NPM-1092456  \n**Package:** npm/sweetalert2  \n**Version:** 11.4.26  \n**Language:** JavaScript (Node.js)\n\nThe vulnerability identified as NPM-1092456 in the `sweetalert2` package version 11.4.26 is a security flaw that could potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or perform unauthorized actions. This vulnerability arises from improper input validation or insufficient sanitization of user inputs, which can lead to cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks or other injection-based attacks.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade Package:** The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a version of `sweetalert2` where this vulnerability is patched. Check the [official repository](https://github.com/sweetalert2/sweetalert2) for the latest secure version.\n\n2. **Input Validation:** Implement strict input validation and sanitization to ensure that all user inputs are properly checked and cleaned before processing.\n\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP):** Implement a strong Content Security Policy to mitigate the impact of XSS attacks by restricting the sources from which scripts can be loaded.\n\n4. **Regular Audits:** Conduct regular security audits and dependency checks to identify and address vulnerabilities in third-party packages.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a temporary fix by manually sanitizing inputs and ensuring that any dynamic content rendered by `sweetalert2` is properly escaped. For example:\n\n```javascript\nimport Swal from 'sweetalert2';\n\n// Example of escaping user input\nconst userInput = '<script>alert(\"XSS\")</script>';\nconst sanitizedInput = userInput.replace(/</g, \"&lt;\").replace(/>/g, \"&gt;\");\n\nSwal.fire({\n  title: 'User Input',\n  text: sanitizedInput,\n  icon: 'info'\n});\n```\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://owasp.org/www-project-cheat-sheets/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "NPM-1092456/pkg:npm/sweetalert2@11.4.26"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-6a912911-b6b5-481e-a131-0a6d1f3221e8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2021-41183** affects the `jquery-ui` package, specifically version `1.10.4`. This vulnerability is related to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) due to improper input sanitization in certain components of the library. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by injecting malicious scripts into web pages that use the affected version of `jquery-ui`, potentially leading to unauthorized actions being performed on behalf of a user or the exposure of sensitive information.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a version of `jquery-ui` that has addressed this vulnerability. Check the official repository or npm registry for the latest secure version.\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation and sanitization on all user inputs to prevent malicious scripts from being executed.\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Use CSP headers to restrict the sources from which scripts can be loaded and executed.\n4. **Security Testing**: Regularly perform security testing, including automated scans and manual reviews, to identify and address vulnerabilities.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a patch to sanitize inputs in the affected components. For example, ensure that any user-generated content is properly escaped before being inserted into the DOM. Here is a general example of how to escape HTML in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\nfunction escapeHtml(unsafe) {\n    return unsafe\n        .replace(/&/g, \"&amp;\")\n        .replace(/</g, \"&lt;\")\n        .replace(/>/g, \"&gt;\")\n        .replace(/\"/g, \"&quot;\")\n        .replace(/'/g, \"&#039;\");\n}\n```\n\nUse this function to sanitize any user input before it is used in the application.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Content Security Policy (CSP) Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2021-41183/pkg:npm/jquery-ui@1.10.4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-38e85883-019c-4b80-b74a-8e8a41f18167",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\nCVE-2015-9251 is a vulnerability found in jQuery versions prior to 3.0.0, including version 1.10.2, which is commonly used in npm packages. This vulnerability is related to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks that can occur when untrusted data is passed to jQuery's `html()` function. The issue arises because jQuery does not properly sanitize the input, allowing attackers to inject malicious scripts into web pages.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade jQuery**: Update to jQuery version 3.0.0 or later, where this vulnerability has been addressed.\r\n2. **Sanitize Inputs**: Ensure that all inputs are properly sanitized and validated before being processed by jQuery functions.\r\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implement a strong Content Security Policy to reduce the risk of XSS attacks.\r\n4. **Review Dependencies**: Regularly review and update all dependencies to their latest secure versions.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\nTo fix this issue in your code, you should upgrade the jQuery library to a version that is not affected by this vulnerability. Update your `package.json` to use a secure version of jQuery:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"dependencies\": {\r\n    \"jquery\": \"^3.0.0\"\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter updating, run `npm install` to apply the changes.\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n- [OWASP Content Security Policy (CSP) Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2015-9251/pkg:npm/jquery@1.10.2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-675158b2-359d-4ea8-8239-e43ad1376066",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability CVE-2016-7103 affects the `jquery-ui` package version 1.10.4, which is a popular set of user interface interactions, effects, widgets, and themes built on top of the jQuery JavaScript Library. This vulnerability is related to cross-site scripting (XSS) due to improper validation of user input in certain components of the library. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by injecting malicious scripts into web pages that use the affected version of `jquery-ui`, potentially leading to unauthorized actions or data exposure.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade**: The most effective way to mitigate this vulnerability is to upgrade to a later version of `jquery-ui` where the issue has been resolved. Check the official repository or package manager for the latest secure version.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation on all user inputs to ensure that they do not contain malicious scripts. Use libraries or frameworks that provide built-in XSS protection.\n\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implement a strong Content Security Policy to restrict the sources from which scripts can be executed.\n\n4. **Sanitize Outputs**: Ensure that all outputs are properly sanitized before being rendered in the browser.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a patch to sanitize inputs and outputs in the affected components. This involves escaping any user-generated content before it is inserted into the DOM. For example, use jQuery's `.text()` method instead of `.html()` to prevent script execution.\n\n```javascript\n// Example of escaping user input\nvar userInput = getUserInput(); // Assume this function gets user input\n$('#element').text(userInput); // Use .text() to prevent XSS\n```\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://owasp.org/www-project-cheat-sheets/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2016-7103/pkg:npm/jquery-ui@1.10.4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-2fe1cc3c-c014-4a3e-a6cc-8cf5d338b38e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**CVE-2021-41182** is a vulnerability identified in the `jquery-ui` package, specifically in version `1.10.4`. This vulnerability arises from improper input validation, which can lead to cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by injecting malicious scripts into web pages that use the affected version of `jquery-ui`, potentially leading to unauthorized actions or data exposure.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a later version of `jquery-ui` where this vulnerability is patched. Check the [official jQuery UI repository](https://github.com/jquery/jquery-ui) for the latest stable release.\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement robust input validation on both client and server sides to ensure that only expected data is processed.\r\n\r\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Use CSP headers to restrict the sources from which scripts can be executed, reducing the risk of XSS.\r\n\r\n4. **Sanitize User Input**: Use libraries or frameworks that automatically escape or sanitize user input to prevent script injection.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a patch to sanitize inputs that are processed by `jquery-ui` components. For example, ensure that any data passed to jQuery UI widgets is properly escaped or sanitized to prevent script execution.\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// Example of sanitizing input before using it in jQuery UI\r\nfunction sanitizeInput(input) {\r\n    return $('<div>').text(input).html();\r\n}\r\n\r\nlet userInput = getUserInput(); // Assume this function fetches user input\r\nlet safeInput = sanitizeInput(userInput);\r\n\r\n// Use safeInput with jQuery UI components\r\n$(\"#element\").text(safeInput);\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n- [OWASP Content Security Policy (CSP) Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- **CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')**\r\n  - [CWE-79 Details](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2021-41182/pkg:npm/jquery-ui@1.10.4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-f3bfba4d-e7e3-48d8-9f8d-b789fa39e476",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\nThe vulnerability CVE-2021-41184 affects the `jquery-ui` package version 1.10.4, which is a popular set of user interface interactions, effects, widgets, and themes built on top of the jQuery JavaScript Library. This vulnerability is related to cross-site scripting (XSS) issues that can occur due to improper validation of user inputs. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by injecting malicious scripts into web pages that use the affected version of `jquery-ui`, potentially leading to unauthorized actions or data exposure.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a later version of `jquery-ui` where this vulnerability is patched. Check the official repository or package manager for the latest secure version.\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation on all user inputs to ensure that they do not contain malicious scripts or code.\r\n\r\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Use CSP headers to restrict the sources from which scripts can be loaded, reducing the risk of XSS attacks.\r\n\r\n4. **Sanitize Outputs**: Ensure that all outputs are properly sanitized before being rendered in the browser.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a patch to sanitize inputs and outputs. For example, you can use a library like DOMPurify to clean any HTML content before it is inserted into the DOM:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// Example of using DOMPurify to sanitize HTML content\r\nvar cleanHTML = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirtyHTML);\r\ndocument.getElementById('output').innerHTML = cleanHTML;\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n- [OWASP Content Security Policy (CSP) Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2021-41184/pkg:npm/jquery-ui@1.10.4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-9e1c1254-c666-4be7-acc4-c807c7b603d3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability CVE-2023-26159 affects the `follow-redirects` package version 1.15.1 in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability arises from improper handling of certain HTTP headers, which can lead to HTTP request smuggling. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability to bypass security controls, access sensitive information, or perform unauthorized actions.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Upgrade the Package**: Update `follow-redirects` to a version where the vulnerability is patched. Check the package repository or security advisories for the latest secure version.\n2. **Review Dependencies**: Ensure that all dependencies are up-to-date and do not include vulnerable versions of `follow-redirects`.\n3. **Implement Security Headers**: Use security headers like `Content-Security-Policy`, `X-Content-Type-Options`, and `X-Frame-Options` to add an additional layer of security.\n4. **Monitor Network Traffic**: Implement monitoring to detect unusual patterns that may indicate an attempt to exploit this vulnerability.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are maintaining a fork or cannot immediately upgrade, consider applying a patch to sanitize and validate HTTP headers before processing them. Ensure that your application correctly handles and validates all incoming HTTP requests to prevent smuggling attacks.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Headers Project](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-headers/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\nThis vulnerability is related to [CWE-444: Inconsistent Interpretation of HTTP Requests ('HTTP Request Smuggling')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/444.html).\n\nPlease ensure to verify the links for accessibility and validity."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2023-26159/pkg:npm/follow-redirects@1.15.1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-a89cd4cd-aefb-4cda-8a3a-8b285f613816",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\nCVE-2022-31129 is a vulnerability identified in the `moment` package, specifically in version 2.24.0. The vulnerability arises from improper handling of certain date and time inputs, which can lead to unexpected behavior or potential security risks in applications that rely on this package for date manipulation. This issue can be exploited to cause denial of service or other unintended application behavior.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to upgrade the `moment` package to the latest version where this issue has been addressed. Regularly updating dependencies is a best practice to ensure that known vulnerabilities are patched.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\nReview your project's `package.json` file and update the `moment` dependency to the latest version. You can do this by running:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nnpm install moment@latest\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter updating, ensure that your application is thoroughly tested to confirm that the update does not introduce any new issues.\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\r\n- [OWASP Dependency-Check](https://owasp.org/www-project-dependency-check/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\nThis vulnerability is related to CWE-20: Improper Input Validation. More information can be found at the following link:\r\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2022-31129/pkg:npm/moment@2.24.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-a550f2b3-75e0-491a-8da7-770c748f71d3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**CVE-2022-24785** is a vulnerability identified in the `moment` package, specifically in version `2.24.0`. This vulnerability arises from improper handling of certain date and time inputs, which can lead to unexpected behavior or potential security risks in applications that rely on this package for date manipulation. The vulnerability may allow attackers to exploit the application by providing specially crafted inputs that can cause the application to behave incorrectly or crash.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade the `moment` package**: Update to the latest version of `moment` where this vulnerability has been addressed. As of the latest information, upgrading to version `2.29.1` or later is advised.\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that all date and time inputs are sanitized and conform to expected formats before processing.\r\n\r\n3. **Monitor Dependencies**: Regularly check for updates and security advisories for all dependencies used in your project. Tools like `npm audit` can help automate this process.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a patch or workaround that addresses the specific issue. This might involve:\r\n\r\n- Implementing custom validation logic to handle edge cases that `moment` does not cover.\r\n- Using alternative libraries that provide similar functionality with better security practices.\r\n\r\n### Relevant Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/): A list of the most critical security risks to web applications.\r\n- [OWASP Dependency-Check](https://owasp.org/www-project-dependency-check/): A tool to identify project dependencies and check if there are any known, publicly disclosed, vulnerabilities.\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html): This CWE is relevant as the vulnerability involves improper handling of input data.\r\n\r\nPlease ensure to verify the links and their accessibility as they may change over time."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2022-24785/pkg:npm/moment@2.24.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-a8b85a79-e385-49ef-9769-bb4426245ff2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability CVE-2023-46233 affects the `crypto-js` package version 4.1.1 in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability arises from improper handling of cryptographic operations, which could potentially lead to information disclosure or other security issues. The vulnerability is particularly concerning in scenarios where sensitive data is being encrypted or decrypted, as it may allow an attacker to gain unauthorized access to this data.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Upgrade the Package**: Update `crypto-js` to a version where this vulnerability is patched. Check the [npm registry](https://www.npmjs.com/package/crypto-js) for the latest secure version.\n2. **Review Cryptographic Practices**: Ensure that cryptographic operations are implemented following best practices. Avoid using deprecated or weak algorithms.\n3. **Monitor Dependencies**: Regularly audit and monitor your project's dependencies for known vulnerabilities using tools like `npm audit` or third-party services such as Snyk.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are unable to upgrade immediately, consider the following temporary workaround:\n\n- **Isolate Vulnerable Code**: Identify and isolate the code that uses `crypto-js` for critical operations. Limit its exposure and ensure that it handles data securely.\n- **Implement Additional Security Measures**: Use additional layers of security, such as input validation and output encoding, to mitigate potential risks.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten 2021](https://owasp.org/Top10/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-327 - Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n\nThese resources provide further guidance on secure cryptographic practices and general security measures to protect your applications."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2023-46233/pkg:npm/crypto-js@4.1.1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-cd731a2d-0185-409a-9565-5db83b752ad0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\nCVE-2024-29881 is a vulnerability identified in the `tinymce` package version 6.8.5, which is a popular open-source WYSIWYG editor used in web applications. This vulnerability arises from improper input validation, leading to potential cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by injecting malicious scripts into the editor, which could then be executed in the context of the user's browser session.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n1. **Upgrade**: The primary mitigation strategy is to upgrade to a patched version of the `tinymce` package. Check the official repository or npm registry for the latest secure version.\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation on all user inputs to ensure that only expected data is processed.\r\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Use CSP headers to restrict the sources from which scripts can be executed.\r\n4. **Sanitize User Input**: Use libraries like DOMPurify to sanitize user inputs and outputs to prevent script execution.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a temporary fix by sanitizing inputs directly in the application code. For example, use a library like DOMPurify to clean any HTML content before it is processed by TinyMCE:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nimport DOMPurify from 'dompurify';\r\n\r\nconst cleanHTML = DOMPurify.sanitize(userInput);\r\ntinymce.init({\r\n  selector: '#editor',\r\n  setup: function (editor) {\r\n    editor.on('change', function () {\r\n      editor.setContent(cleanHTML);\r\n    });\r\n  }\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Relevant Resources\r\n- [OWASP Content Security Policy Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2024-29881/pkg:npm/tinymce@6.8.5"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-0b5b4ed2-50eb-4f44-9b01-8fb6c11e59d7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\nThe vulnerability identified as CVE-2022-3517 affects the `minimatch` package, specifically version 3.0.4, which is commonly used in Node.js applications for matching file paths against specified patterns. This vulnerability arises from improper handling of certain patterns, which can lead to a denial of service (DoS) attack. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by crafting a malicious pattern that causes excessive backtracking, leading to high CPU usage and potential application downtime.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade the `minimatch` package**: Check for newer versions of the `minimatch` package that have addressed this vulnerability. As of the latest information, upgrading to version 3.0.5 or later should resolve the issue.\r\n\r\n2. **Validate Input**: Implement input validation to ensure that only safe and expected patterns are processed by the application.\r\n\r\n3. **Limit Pattern Complexity**: Restrict the complexity of patterns that can be processed to prevent excessive backtracking.\r\n\r\n4. **Monitor Application Performance**: Use monitoring tools to detect unusual CPU usage patterns that may indicate an attempted exploitation of this vulnerability.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a patch to the `minimatch` source code to handle patterns more efficiently. This may involve optimizing the pattern matching logic to reduce backtracking. However, this approach requires a deep understanding of the package's internals and should be approached with caution.\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\r\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet Series](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\nThis vulnerability is related to [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html), which describes issues where a system does not properly control the allocation and management of resources, leading to potential denial of service."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2022-3517/pkg:npm/minimatch@3.0.4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-6b83db0a-dd29-405d-93f4-649f89780c54",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**Vulnerability CVE-2024-27088** affects the `es5-ext` package version `0.10.53` in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability is identified in the OSS (Open Source Software) programming language environment. The issue arises from improper input validation, which can lead to potential security risks such as arbitrary code execution or denial of service. The vulnerability is particularly concerning in environments where untrusted input is processed.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade**: The primary mitigation strategy is to upgrade to a patched version of the `es5-ext` package. Check the package repository or advisory for the latest secure version.\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation and sanitization to prevent malicious data from being processed.\r\n3. **Security Audits**: Regularly perform security audits and dependency checks to identify and mitigate vulnerabilities in third-party packages.\r\n4. **Monitoring**: Use runtime application self-protection (RASP) tools to monitor and block potential exploit attempts.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nTo address the vulnerability, consider the following general steps:\r\n\r\n1. **Review Code**: Examine the codebase for areas where input is processed without validation.\r\n2. **Implement Validation**: Add validation checks to ensure that inputs conform to expected formats and types.\r\n3. **Test**: Thoroughly test the application after making changes to ensure that the fix does not introduce new issues.\r\n\r\n### Relevant Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/): A list of the most critical web application security risks.\r\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/): A quick reference guide for secure coding practices.\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- **CWE-20: Improper Input Validation**: This vulnerability is closely related to CWE-20, which involves the failure to properly validate input, leading to potential security risks.\r\n\r\nFor more information on CWE-20, visit the [CWE website](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2024-27088/pkg:npm/es5-ext@0.10.53"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-e9412288-e47a-4294-b2aa-a7bc35032377",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**Vulnerability CVE-2024-28849** affects the `follow-redirects` package version `1.15.1` in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability arises from improper handling of HTTP redirects, which can lead to potential security issues such as open redirect attacks or exposure of sensitive information. The vulnerability is particularly concerning in applications that rely on `follow-redirects` for handling HTTP requests, as it may allow an attacker to manipulate redirect URLs to malicious endpoints.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade the Package**: The primary mitigation strategy is to upgrade `follow-redirects` to a version where the vulnerability is patched. Check the package repository or security advisories for the latest secure version.\r\n\r\n2. **Validate Redirects**: Implement additional validation for redirect URLs in your application. Ensure that redirects are only allowed to trusted domains.\r\n\r\n3. **Use Security Headers**: Implement security headers such as `Content-Security-Policy` and `X-Content-Type-Options` to mitigate the impact of potential attacks.\r\n\r\n4. **Monitor Dependencies**: Regularly audit and monitor your project's dependencies for known vulnerabilities using tools like `npm audit` or `Snyk`.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nTo address this vulnerability, consider the following code changes:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nconst followRedirects = require('follow-redirects');\r\n\r\n// Example of validating redirect URLs\r\nfunction isValidRedirect(url) {\r\n  const allowedDomains = ['https://trusted.com', 'https://another-trusted.com'];\r\n  return allowedDomains.some(domain => url.startsWith(domain));\r\n}\r\n\r\nfollowRedirects.http.get('http://example.com', (response) => {\r\n  if (isValidRedirect(response.headers.location)) {\r\n    // Proceed with the redirect\r\n  } else {\r\n    // Handle invalid redirect\r\n    console.error('Invalid redirect URL');\r\n  }\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Relevant Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/): A list of the top ten security risks for web applications.\r\n- [OWASP Secure Headers Project](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-headers/): Information on implementing security headers.\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- **CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')**: This vulnerability is closely related to CWE-601, which involves improper handling of URL redirects.\r\n\r\nFor more information on CWE-601, visit the [CWE website](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2024-28849/pkg:npm/follow-redirects@1.15.1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-71548075-7894-482b-b14e-ac72962c488d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**Vulnerability CVE-2023-45857** affects the `axios` package version `0.26.1` in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability is related to improper handling of user input, which can lead to a potential security risk such as a Denial of Service (DoS) or other unintended behaviors. The issue arises from insufficient validation or sanitization of input data, which can be exploited by an attacker to cause unexpected application behavior or crash the application.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade the `axios` package**: Update to the latest version of `axios` where this vulnerability is patched. You can do this by running:\r\n   ```bash\r\n   npm install axios@latest\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation and sanitization to ensure that all user inputs are properly checked before processing.\r\n\r\n3. **Monitor Dependencies**: Regularly check for updates and security advisories for all dependencies in your project.\r\n\r\n4. **Use Security Tools**: Employ tools like `npm audit` to automatically detect vulnerabilities in your dependencies.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nIf you are unable to upgrade `axios` immediately, consider applying a temporary fix by adding input validation checks in your code where `axios` is used. For example:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nconst axios = require('axios');\r\n\r\nfunction isValidInput(input) {\r\n  // Implement your validation logic here\r\n  return typeof input === 'string' && input.length > 0;\r\n}\r\n\r\nasync function fetchData(url) {\r\n  if (!isValidInput(url)) {\r\n    throw new Error('Invalid URL');\r\n  }\r\n  try {\r\n    const response = await axios.get(url);\r\n    return response.data;\r\n  } catch (error) {\r\n    console.error('Error fetching data:', error);\r\n    throw error;\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Relevant Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2023-45857/pkg:npm/axios@0.26.1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-4c739a64-4cb8-49de-9abe-794560de4da3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\nCVE-2024-4068 is a vulnerability identified in the `braces` package, version 2.3.2, which is commonly used in Node.js applications. This vulnerability arises from improper input validation, leading to a potential Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) attack. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by providing a specially crafted input that causes the regular expression engine to consume excessive CPU resources, leading to a denial of service.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n1. **Upgrade the Package**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade the `braces` package to a version where this vulnerability is patched. Check the package repository or advisories for the latest secure version.\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that only expected and safe inputs are processed by the application.\r\n3. **Rate Limiting**: Implement rate limiting to reduce the impact of potential denial of service attacks.\r\n4. **Monitoring and Alerts**: Set up monitoring and alerting to detect unusual patterns of resource consumption that may indicate an ongoing attack.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\nTo address this vulnerability, you should update the `braces` package to a version where the issue is resolved. If an update is not immediately possible, consider the following temporary workaround:\r\n\r\n- Review and refactor any code that uses the `braces` package to ensure that it does not process untrusted input directly.\r\n- Consider using alternative libraries that provide similar functionality but are not affected by this vulnerability.\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten: A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\r\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet: Input Validation](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet: Denial of Service](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Denial_of_Service_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption ('Resource Exhaustion')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2024-4068/pkg:npm/braces@2.3.2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-a09cd11a-e5f5-4f78-8fa0-cbe12529cffb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**Vulnerability CVE-2024-37629** affects the `summernote` package version `0.8.20` in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability is related to improper input validation, which can lead to cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by injecting malicious scripts into the content processed by `summernote`, potentially leading to unauthorized actions or data exposure when the content is rendered in a web browser.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider the following steps:\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade**: Update `summernote` to a version where this vulnerability is patched. Check the [official repository](https://github.com/summernote/summernote) for the latest secure version.\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation and sanitization on all user inputs before processing them with `summernote`.\r\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Use CSP headers to restrict the execution of scripts and reduce the risk of XSS attacks.\r\n4. **Security Libraries**: Utilize libraries such as DOMPurify to sanitize HTML content and prevent XSS.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nIf you are maintaining a fork or cannot upgrade immediately, consider applying a patch to sanitize inputs before they are processed by `summernote`. For example, integrate a library like DOMPurify:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nconst DOMPurify = require('dompurify');\r\n\r\n// Example of sanitizing input before using it in summernote\r\nconst cleanInput = DOMPurify.sanitize(userInput);\r\n$('#summernote').summernote({\r\n  // other configurations\r\n  callbacks: {\r\n    onChange: function(contents, $editable) {\r\n      const sanitizedContent = DOMPurify.sanitize(contents);\r\n      // Use sanitizedContent safely\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n- [OWASP Content Security Policy (CSP) Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\nThis vulnerability is related to [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2024-37629/pkg:npm/summernote@0.8.20"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-c05415d8-529e-4e09-8305-b94c7240f624",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\nCVE-2024-4067 is a vulnerability identified in the `micromatch` package, specifically in version 3.1.10. This vulnerability affects the way the package processes certain patterns, potentially leading to a Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS). An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by providing a specially crafted input that causes the regular expression engine to consume excessive CPU resources, leading to a denial of service condition.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to upgrade to a patched version of the `micromatch` package. If an upgrade is not immediately possible, consider implementing input validation to limit the complexity of patterns processed by the application. Additionally, consider using a timeout mechanism to abort long-running regular expression operations.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\nThe fix for this vulnerability involves optimizing the regular expressions used within the `micromatch` package to prevent excessive backtracking. This can be achieved by simplifying the regular expressions or using more efficient pattern matching techniques. It is recommended to review the changes made in the patched version of the package for specific details on the fix.\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten: A6:2017-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\r\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet: Input Validation](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet: Denial of Service](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Denial_of_Service_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption ('Resource Exhaustion')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2024-4067/pkg:npm/micromatch@3.1.10"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-0727f7b4-121a-49c8-b638-fd1ab609f124",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**Vulnerability CVE-2024-21489** affects the `uplot` package version `1.6.18` in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability is identified in the OSS programming language and is classified as a security flaw that could potentially allow attackers to exploit the package in a way that compromises the integrity, confidentiality, or availability of the application using it. The specific nature of the vulnerability could involve improper input validation, leading to potential cross-site scripting (XSS) or other injection attacks.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade the Package**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade `uplot` to a version where the vulnerability is patched. Check the package repository or security advisories for the latest secure version.\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation and sanitization to prevent malicious data from being processed by the application.\r\n\r\n3. **Use Security Libraries**: Employ libraries or frameworks that automatically handle input sanitization and output encoding.\r\n\r\n4. **Regular Audits**: Conduct regular security audits and code reviews to identify and address potential vulnerabilities.\r\n\r\n5. **Security Headers**: Implement security headers such as Content Security Policy (CSP) to mitigate the risk of XSS attacks.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a patch to sanitize inputs and outputs. For example, ensure that any data rendered by `uplot` is properly escaped or encoded to prevent script execution.\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// Example of escaping potentially dangerous characters\r\nfunction escapeHTML(str) {\r\n  return str.replace(/[&<>\"']/g, function (match) {\r\n    const escapeMap = {\r\n      '&': '&amp;',\r\n      '<': '&lt;',\r\n      '>': '&gt;',\r\n      '\"': '&quot;',\r\n      \"'\": '&#39;'\r\n    };\r\n    return escapeMap[match];\r\n  });\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Usage\r\nconst safeData = escapeHTML(userInput);\r\n```\r\n\r\n### OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\r\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet Series](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2024-21489/pkg:npm/uplot@1.6.18"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-dfe81d9c-123f-49fd-92eb-1592bec2a8f5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\nThe vulnerability identified as CVE-2022-31160 affects the `jquery-ui` package, specifically version `1.10.4`, which is commonly used in web applications for creating interactive user interfaces. This vulnerability is related to cross-site scripting (XSS) issues, where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. This can lead to unauthorized actions being performed on behalf of the user, data theft, or other malicious activities.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a later version of `jquery-ui` where this vulnerability has been addressed. Check the official repository or npm registry for the latest secure version.\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation on all user inputs to ensure that no malicious scripts can be injected.\r\n\r\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Use CSP headers to restrict the sources from which scripts can be executed, reducing the risk of XSS attacks.\r\n\r\n4. **Sanitize Outputs**: Ensure that all outputs are properly sanitized before being rendered in the browser.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a patch to sanitize inputs and outputs. For example, use libraries like `DOMPurify` to clean any HTML content before it is inserted into the DOM.\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// Example of using DOMPurify to sanitize HTML\r\nconst cleanHTML = DOMPurify.sanitize(userInput);\r\ndocument.getElementById('output').innerHTML = cleanHTML;\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Relevant Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://owasp.org/www-project-cheat-sheets/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2022-31160/pkg:npm/jquery-ui@1.10.4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-c155955b-499b-419f-909b-ee8ea678c144",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\n**CVE-2019-11358** is a vulnerability found in jQuery versions prior to 3.4.0, including `pkg npm/jquery@1.10.2`. This vulnerability arises from the improper handling of `jQuery.extend` and `jQuery.fn.extend` functions, which can lead to prototype pollution. Prototype pollution allows an attacker to inject properties into JavaScript objects, potentially leading to denial of service (DoS) or other unexpected behaviors in applications that rely on these objects.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade jQuery**: The most effective way to mitigate this vulnerability is to upgrade to jQuery version 3.4.0 or later, where this issue has been addressed.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that only expected and safe data is processed by your application.\n\n3. **Security Audits**: Regularly perform security audits and code reviews to identify and address potential vulnerabilities in your codebase.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a patch to sanitize inputs to `jQuery.extend` and `jQuery.fn.extend` functions. However, this is a temporary measure and upgrading should be prioritized.\n\n```javascript\n// Example of a patch to prevent prototype pollution\nfunction safeExtend(target, source) {\n    for (let key in source) {\n        if (source.hasOwnProperty(key) && key !== '__proto__' && key !== 'constructor' && key !== 'prototype') {\n            target[key] = source[key];\n        }\n    }\n    return target;\n}\n\n// Usage\nlet obj = {};\nsafeExtend(obj, { key: 'value' });\n```\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet Series](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration\n\n- [CWE-1321: Improperly Controlled Modification of Object Prototype Attributes ('Prototype Pollution')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1321.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2019-11358/pkg:npm/jquery@1.10.2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-1b4d09a1-3424-4857-ab22-0da706ff23aa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**Vulnerability CVE-2022-25883** affects the `semver` package version 5.7.1 in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability arises from improper input validation, which can lead to potential security risks such as denial of service or arbitrary code execution. The issue is particularly concerning in environments where untrusted input is processed, as it may allow attackers to exploit the vulnerability to compromise the application.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade the Package**: The most effective way to mitigate this vulnerability is to upgrade to a patched version of the `semver` package. Check the npm registry for the latest secure version and update your `package.json` file accordingly.\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that only expected and safe data is processed by your application. This can help prevent exploitation of vulnerabilities related to improper input handling.\r\n\r\n3. **Security Audits**: Regularly perform security audits of your dependencies using tools like `npm audit` to identify and address vulnerabilities promptly.\r\n\r\n4. **Monitor Dependencies**: Use tools like `Snyk` or `Dependabot` to automatically monitor and update your dependencies for known vulnerabilities.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nTo address this vulnerability, you should update the `semver` package to a version that has patched the issue. Modify your `package.json` to specify a secure version:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"dependencies\": {\r\n    \"semver\": \"^7.3.5\"\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter updating the `package.json`, run the following command to install the updated package:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nnpm install\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Relevant Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\r\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\nThis vulnerability is related to [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2022-25883/pkg:npm/semver@5.7.1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-b89377a2-3ce0-43f0-9421-8478d5917053",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\nThe vulnerability identified as CVE-2022-48285 affects the `jszip` package version 3.7.1 in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability arises from improper handling of zip file entries, which can lead to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by crafting a malicious zip file that, when processed by the vulnerable `jszip` version, could cause the application to crash or become unresponsive.\r\n\r\n### General Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade the Package**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade `jszip` to a version where this vulnerability is patched. Check the [jszip repository](https://github.com/Stuk/jszip) for the latest secure version.\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that only well-formed zip files are processed by your application.\r\n\r\n3. **Limit Resource Usage**: Consider implementing resource limits (e.g., memory, CPU) for processes handling zip files to mitigate the impact of potential DoS attacks.\r\n\r\n4. **Monitor and Alert**: Set up monitoring and alerting for unusual application behavior that could indicate an attempted exploitation of this vulnerability.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nTo address this vulnerability, ensure that your `package.json` specifies a secure version of `jszip`. Update your dependencies by running:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nnpm install jszip@latest\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis command will update `jszip` to the latest version, which should include the necessary security patches.\r\n\r\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Dependency-Check](https://owasp.org/www-project-dependency-check/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\nThis vulnerability is related to [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption ('Resource Exhaustion')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2022-48285/pkg:npm/jszip@3.7.1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-765f1210-e16f-4732-967b-70e196345fa8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nCVE-2020-11023 is a Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability found in jQuery versions prior to 3.5.0. This vulnerability arises from the improper handling of HTML content, which can lead to the execution of arbitrary JavaScript code in the context of the user's browser. This can be exploited by an attacker to perform actions such as stealing session tokens, redirecting users to malicious sites, or performing actions on behalf of the user.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Upgrade jQuery**: Update to jQuery version 3.5.0 or later, where this vulnerability has been addressed.\n2. **Sanitize Inputs**: Ensure that any user inputs that are incorporated into HTML content are properly sanitized and escaped.\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implement a strong Content Security Policy to reduce the risk of XSS attacks.\n4. **Review and Test**: Regularly review and test your codebase for potential XSS vulnerabilities.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are unable to upgrade jQuery immediately, consider applying a temporary fix by sanitizing inputs and avoiding the use of functions that directly inject HTML content without proper validation. However, upgrading is the most reliable solution.\n\n### Relevant Links\n\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Content Security Policy (CSP) Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-79](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2020-11023/pkg:npm/jquery@1.10.2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-292f61b9-f4b3-4050-9cd1-35ea5058fe43",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\n**CVE-2020-11022** is a Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability found in jQuery versions prior to 3.5.0. This vulnerability arises from the improper handling of HTML content in the `jQuery.htmlPrefilter` method, which can lead to the execution of arbitrary JavaScript code when untrusted data is passed to jQuery's DOM manipulation methods. This can potentially allow attackers to execute scripts in the context of the user's browser, leading to data theft, session hijacking, or other malicious activities.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade jQuery**: The most effective way to mitigate this vulnerability is to upgrade to jQuery version 3.5.0 or later, where this issue has been addressed.\n\n2. **Sanitize Inputs**: Ensure that any data being passed to jQuery's DOM manipulation methods is properly sanitized and validated to prevent the injection of malicious scripts.\n\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implement a strong Content Security Policy to reduce the risk of XSS attacks by restricting the sources from which scripts can be loaded.\n\n4. **Review and Refactor Code**: Audit your codebase to identify and refactor any instances where untrusted data is being passed to jQuery methods that manipulate the DOM.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf upgrading jQuery is not immediately possible, consider applying a temporary patch by overriding the `jQuery.htmlPrefilter` method to sanitize inputs. However, this is not a substitute for upgrading and should only be used as a stopgap measure.\n\n```javascript\n// Example of overriding jQuery.htmlPrefilter\njQuery.htmlPrefilter = function(html) {\n    // Implement a basic sanitization logic\n    return html.replace(/<script/gi, \"&lt;script\");\n};\n```\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Content Security Policy (CSP) Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2020-11022/pkg:npm/jquery@1.10.2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-e091c502-e4c4-4c14-85f5-392f58e8f822",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**Vulnerability CVE-2025-3573** affects the `jquery-validation` package version `1.19.5` in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability arises from improper input validation, which can lead to cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by injecting malicious scripts into form fields, which are then executed in the context of the user's browser session.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade**: The primary mitigation strategy is to upgrade to a patched version of `jquery-validation`. Check the package repository or npm advisories for the latest secure version.\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement server-side input validation to ensure that all user inputs are sanitized and validated before processing.\r\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implement a strong CSP to mitigate the impact of XSS attacks by restricting the sources from which scripts can be executed.\r\n4. **Escaping Outputs**: Ensure that all outputs are properly escaped, especially when rendering user-generated content.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nReview the code where user inputs are processed and ensure that all inputs are validated and sanitized. For example, use libraries like DOMPurify to sanitize HTML inputs:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nconst DOMPurify = require('dompurify');\r\n\r\n// Example of sanitizing user input\r\nconst userInput = '<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>';\r\nconst sanitizedInput = DOMPurify.sanitize(userInput);\r\n```\r\n\r\n### OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n- [OWASP Content Security Policy (CSP) Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2025-3573/pkg:npm/jquery-validation@1.19.5"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-770de811-fc68-4ae3-8e5b-14081c5bc1fe",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**Vulnerability CVE-2025-27152** affects the `axios` package version `0.26.1` in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability is related to improper handling of user input, which can lead to a potential security risk such as a Denial of Service (DoS) or Remote Code Execution (RCE). The issue arises from insufficient validation of input data, allowing attackers to exploit the application by sending specially crafted requests.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade**: Update `axios` to the latest version where this vulnerability is patched. Check the [axios GitHub repository](https://github.com/axios/axios) for the latest releases and update your `package.json` accordingly.\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation and sanitization to ensure that all incoming data is properly checked before processing.\r\n\r\n3. **Security Headers**: Use security headers to protect against common vulnerabilities. Consider using libraries like `helmet` for Express.js applications.\r\n\r\n4. **Rate Limiting**: Implement rate limiting to prevent abuse of the application by limiting the number of requests a user can make in a given time frame.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a temporary fix by adding input validation checks in your application code. For example:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nconst axios = require('axios');\r\n\r\n// Example of input validation\r\nfunction isValidInput(input) {\r\n  // Implement validation logic\r\n  return typeof input === 'string' && input.length < 100;\r\n}\r\n\r\nasync function fetchData(url) {\r\n  if (!isValidInput(url)) {\r\n    throw new Error('Invalid input');\r\n  }\r\n  try {\r\n    const response = await axios.get(url);\r\n    return response.data;\r\n  } catch (error) {\r\n    console.error('Error fetching data:', error);\r\n    throw error;\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### References\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\r\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\nThis vulnerability is related to [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2025-27152/pkg:npm/axios@0.26.1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-2301ff09-c226-414a-a804-4078dcaa9ad2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\nThe vulnerability identified as CVE-2023-26115 affects the `word-wrap` package version 1.2.3 in the npm ecosystem. This vulnerability arises from improper handling of input data, which can lead to potential security risks such as Denial of Service (DoS) or other unexpected behaviors. The issue is primarily due to insufficient input validation, allowing attackers to exploit the package by providing specially crafted input that can cause the application to behave unexpectedly or crash.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade the Package**: Check for any newer versions of the `word-wrap` package that have addressed this vulnerability. If a patched version is available, upgrade to it as soon as possible.\r\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that only expected and safe data is processed by the application.\r\n3. **Monitor Dependencies**: Regularly audit and monitor your project's dependencies for known vulnerabilities using tools like `npm audit` or `Snyk`.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\nIf upgrading the package is not immediately possible, consider applying a temporary fix by modifying the source code to include additional input validation checks. For example, ensure that input strings are sanitized and validated before being processed by the `word-wrap` function.\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nfunction safeWordWrap(input, options) {\r\n  if (typeof input !== 'string') {\r\n    throw new Error('Input must be a string');\r\n  }\r\n  // Additional validation logic can be added here\r\n  return wordWrap(input, options);\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Relevant Resources\r\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\r\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2023-26115/pkg:npm/word-wrap@1.2.3"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-5db4048c-b217-4df5-b7a1-567afb203f79",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description of \"Unsecured URL\" Vulnerability in C# Programming Language\n\nThe \"Unsecured URL\" vulnerability occurs when an application uses URLs that are not secured with HTTPS, potentially exposing sensitive data to interception or tampering by attackers. In C#, this can happen when making HTTP requests using libraries like `HttpClient` without ensuring the URL is secure. This vulnerability can lead to man-in-the-middle attacks, data breaches, and other security issues.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Use HTTPS**: Always use HTTPS URLs to ensure data is encrypted during transmission.\n2. **Validate Certificates**: Ensure that SSL/TLS certificates are valid and properly configured.\n3. **Security Headers**: Implement security headers like `Strict-Transport-Security` to enforce HTTPS.\n4. **Regular Audits**: Regularly audit your codebase for unsecured URLs and update them to use HTTPS.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nBelow is an example of how to fix an unsecured URL vulnerability in C# by using HTTPS:\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Net.Http;\nusing System.Threading.Tasks;\n\nclass Program\n{\n    static async Task Main()\n    {\n        // Original insecure URL\n        // string url = \"http://www.sitemaps.org\";\n\n        // Secure URL\n        string url = \"https://www.sitemaps.org\";\n\n        using (HttpClient client = new HttpClient())\n        {\n            try\n            {\n                HttpResponseMessage response = await client.GetAsync(url);\n                response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();\n                string responseBody = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();\n                Console.WriteLine(responseBody);\n            }\n            catch (HttpRequestException e)\n            {\n                Console.WriteLine($\"Request error: {e.Message}\");\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code example, you need the following library:\n\n- `System.Net.Http`: This is part of the .NET Standard Library and does not require additional installation if you are using a compatible version of .NET.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Secure Headers Project](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-headers/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-f24734a4-6843-4d74-8eb1-b4ad92b25bdf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description of \"Unsecured URL\" Vulnerability in C# Programming Language\n\nThe \"Unsecured URL\" vulnerability occurs when an application uses URLs that are not secured with HTTPS, potentially exposing sensitive data to interception or tampering by attackers. In C#, this can happen when making HTTP requests using libraries like `HttpClient` without ensuring the URL is secure. This vulnerability can lead to man-in-the-middle attacks, data breaches, and other security issues.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Use HTTPS**: Always use HTTPS URLs to ensure data is encrypted during transmission.\n2. **Validate Certificates**: Ensure that SSL/TLS certificates are valid and properly configured.\n3. **Security Headers**: Implement security headers like `Strict-Transport-Security` to enforce HTTPS.\n4. **Regular Audits**: Regularly audit your codebase for unsecured URLs and update them to use HTTPS.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nBelow is an example of how to fix an unsecured URL vulnerability in C# by using HTTPS:\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Net.Http;\nusing System.Threading.Tasks;\n\nclass Program\n{\n    static async Task Main()\n    {\n        // Original insecure URL\n        // string url = \"http://www.sitemaps.org\";\n\n        // Secure URL\n        string url = \"https://www.sitemaps.org\";\n\n        using (HttpClient client = new HttpClient())\n        {\n            try\n            {\n                HttpResponseMessage response = await client.GetAsync(url);\n                response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();\n                string responseBody = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();\n                Console.WriteLine(responseBody);\n            }\n            catch (HttpRequestException e)\n            {\n                Console.WriteLine($\"Request error: {e.Message}\");\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code example, you need the following library:\n\n- `System.Net.Http`: This is part of the .NET Standard Library and does not require additional installation if you are using a compatible version of .NET.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Secure Headers Project](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-headers/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-b964f173-60ae-4322-b819-4732a17dc806",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\r\n\r\n**Vulnerability CVE-2024-6531** affects the `pkg npm/bootstrap@4.6.2` package. This vulnerability is identified as a Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) issue, which allows attackers to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. This can lead to unauthorized actions being performed on behalf of the user, data theft, or session hijacking.\r\n\r\n### Mitigation Advice\r\n\r\n1. **Upgrade Bootstrap**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a version of Bootstrap where this vulnerability is patched. Check the official Bootstrap repository or npm registry for the latest secure version.\r\n\r\n2. **Input Validation and Sanitization**: Ensure that all user inputs are properly validated and sanitized. Use libraries like DOMPurify to clean HTML inputs.\r\n\r\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implement a strong Content Security Policy to restrict the sources from which scripts can be executed.\r\n\r\n4. **HTTPOnly and Secure Cookies**: Ensure cookies are set with the `HttpOnly` and `Secure` flags to prevent access via JavaScript and ensure they are only sent over HTTPS.\r\n\r\n5. **Regular Security Audits**: Conduct regular security audits and code reviews to identify and fix potential vulnerabilities.\r\n\r\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\r\n\r\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, consider applying a patch to sanitize inputs and outputs. For example, ensure that any data rendered in the UI is properly escaped:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nfunction escapeHtml(unsafe) {\r\n    return unsafe\r\n        .replace(/&/g, \"&amp;\")\r\n        .replace(/</g, \"&lt;\")\r\n        .replace(/>/g, \"&gt;\")\r\n        .replace(/\"/g, \"&quot;\")\r\n        .replace(/'/g, \"&#039;\");\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Usage\r\nconst userInput = getUserInput();\r\nconst safeOutput = escapeHtml(userInput);\r\ndocument.getElementById('output').innerHTML = safeOutput;\r\n```\r\n\r\n### OWASP Resources\r\n\r\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n- [OWASP Content Security Policy (CSP) Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html)\r\n\r\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\r\n\r\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2024-6531/pkg:npm/bootstrap@4.6.2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-153a57e3-58d7-4701-9bfd-d86fc8e91d29",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description of \"Unsecured URL\" Vulnerability in C# Programming Language\n\nThe \"Unsecured URL\" vulnerability occurs when an application uses URLs that are not secured with HTTPS, potentially exposing sensitive data to interception or tampering by attackers. In C#, this can happen when making HTTP requests using libraries like `HttpClient` without ensuring the URL is secure. This vulnerability can lead to man-in-the-middle attacks, data breaches, and other security issues.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Use HTTPS**: Always use HTTPS URLs to ensure data is encrypted during transmission.\n2. **Validate Certificates**: Ensure that SSL/TLS certificates are valid and properly configured.\n3. **Security Headers**: Implement security headers like `Strict-Transport-Security` to enforce HTTPS.\n4. **Regular Audits**: Regularly audit your codebase for unsecured URLs and update them to use HTTPS.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nBelow is an example of how to fix an unsecured URL vulnerability in C# by using HTTPS:\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Net.Http;\nusing System.Threading.Tasks;\n\nclass Program\n{\n    static async Task Main()\n    {\n        // Original insecure URL\n        // string url = \"http://www.sitemaps.org\";\n\n        // Secure URL\n        string url = \"https://www.sitemaps.org\";\n\n        using (HttpClient client = new HttpClient())\n        {\n            try\n            {\n                HttpResponseMessage response = await client.GetAsync(url);\n                response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();\n                string responseBody = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();\n                Console.WriteLine(responseBody);\n            }\n            catch (HttpRequestException e)\n            {\n                Console.WriteLine($\"Request error: {e.Message}\");\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code example, you need the following library:\n\n- `System.Net.Http`: This is part of the .NET Standard Library and does not require additional installation if you are using a compatible version of .NET.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Secure Headers Project](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-headers/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
